[storyguide]
guide1title=Getting started with\nDynamic Settlements
guide1objective=You feel very weak. Open your Journal in the Dynamic Settlements category to learn how to get strong.\n\nPress this button to acknowledge.
guide1button=Proceed
guide2title=Primitive tools
guide2objective=These quests and journal entries will guide you through some of the new mechanics in Dynamic Settlements.\n\nTo get started, interact [input=mouse2] with one of the [item=surfacerocksmall] small rocks scattered around the surface.\n\nAlso, you might want to look for some berries right away.
guide3title=Sharpen the stone
guide3objective=Obtain a [item=sharpenedstone] sharpened stone by striking [input=mouse1] a [item=surfacerocksmall] [item=surfacerock] surface rock with a [item=stone] stone.
guide4title=Obtain wood shafts
guide4objective=Hit [input=mouse1] a tree with a [item=sharpenedstone] sharpened stone to obtain some [item=woodenshaft] wooden shafts.
guide5title=Obtain some grass
guide5objective=Cut some [item=grass] surface grasses with a [item=sharpenedstone] sharpened stone.
guide6title=Craft strings
guide6objective=With a [item=sharpenedstone] sharpened stone in hand, interact [input=mouse2] with any tree stump to use it as a crude workbench.\n\nUse the [item=grass] grass you cut to craft [item=string] strings.
guide7title=Craft a crude axe
guide7objective=Use the crude workbench to craft a [item=crudeaxe] crude axe.
guide8title=Obtain some logs
guide8objective=Cut a few trees with your [item=crudeaxe] crude axe.
guide9title=Obtain some planks
guide9objective=With some <logicon> logs in hand, place them [input=mouse2] on the ground, then use your axe [input=mouse2] on them to cut some <plankicon> planks.\n\nThe same process can be used to cut planks into shafts.
guide10title=Craft some tools
guide10objective=Craft a [item=woodsword] wooden sword to protect yourself.\n\nYou will also need to craft a [item=woodshovel] wooden shovel.
guide11title=Make a hole
guide11objective=Use [input=mouse1] your [item=woodshovel] shovel to uncover dirt, then dig again twice with [input=mouse2] to make a [item=shallowholetile] Shallow Hole.\n\nThese holes have many uses. You can use them for crafting, for accessing caves, and to protect yourself in a pinch.\n\nYou can also simply dig once for collecting dirt, which will be useful in the future. Grass won't be growing on these fine dirt tiles, but they will slowly recover over a period of a couple of days.
guide12title=Make a trapdoor
guide12objective=Craft a [item=trapdoor] trapdoor in your workbench. You can put this trapdoor over a [item=shallowholetile] shallow hole to create a hiding spot from enemies. However, you will need to remove the trapdoor with an axe if you want to use the hole for other purposes.
guide13title=Find some Firemone
guide13objective=Look for some [item=firemone] Firemone flowers. They spawn in forest biomes, you should be able to find some nearby.
guide14title=Fire!
guide14objective=Craft a [item=firestarter] firestarter.
guide15title=Make some crude torches
guide15objective=You will need some [item=crudetorch] crude torches soon, for cave explorations. You need [item=firemone] Firemone flowers to make them. To light a crude torch, you'll need to have a [item=firestarter] firestarter somewhere in your inventory. But remember these torches will last about a day and night cycle before going out. Don't get yourself stranded in the darkness.
guide16title=Craft a crude pickaxe
guide16objective=Craft a [item=crudepickaxe] crude pickaxe in your workbench.
guide17title=Make a cave ladder
guide17objective=Craft a [item=holecaveladder] Cave Ladder in your workbench. You can put the ladder in the [item=shallowholetile] shallow hole that you dug. You can remove the ladder from the hole at any time.
guide18title=Go spelunking
guide18objective=Jump into the hole you dug, and [input=mouse2] interact with it with a [item=crudepickaxe] pickaxe to dig further down. But only do this after you have placed a [item=holecaveladder] ladder in the hole. You'll regret it if you forget.
guide19title=Dig some clay
guide19objective=Inside a cave you can find some [item=clayrock] clay walls. Dig 16 pieces of [item=clay] clay with your [item=woodshovel] shovel.
guide20title=Make some bricks and molds
guide20objective=Use the [item=clay] clay you found to make 8 [item=unfiredbrick] Unfired Bricks. Also make a [item=unfiredsawblademold] Saw Blade Mold, a [item=unfiredthickplatemold] Thick Plate Mold, and other molds you may want later on.
guide21title=Let's cook some food
guide21objective=Craft a [item=campfire] campfire and a [item=roastingstation] roasting station.\n\nRemember that, unlike forges, cooking stations require logs for fuel.
guide22title=Let's cook some clay
guide22objective=You have a new Journal entry for Dynamic Settlements. These will help guide you through some mechanics.\n\nTo complete this objective, complete the new Journal entry.
guide23title=Let's cook some metal
guide23objective=Craft a [item=forge] forge.\n\nForges require charcoal to run. Use the crafting guide book to see what you can make with each mold. For now, make a [item=thickironplate] thick iron plate, and a [item=sawblade] saw blade.
guide24title=Let's work with metal
guide24objective=Make a Crude Anvil by placing [input=mouse2] a [item=thickironplate] Thick Iron Plate over a tree stump.\n\nYou don't have a hammer yet, so you'll have to make do. With a [item=stone] stone in hand, interact [input=mouse2] with the Crude Anvil to use it.\n\nYou can retrieve your Thick Iron Plate at any time.
guide25title=Let's cook together
guide25objective=Complete the new Journal Entry to proceed.
guide26title=Make a proper anvil
guide26objective=Craft an [item=ironanvil] Iron Anvil.
guide27title=Make a saw
guide27objective=Craft a [item=saw] Saw in your new [item=ironanvil] Iron Anvil.
guide27atitle=Make a proper workstation
guide27aobjective=Craft a [item=workstationduo] Workstation from your Crude Workstation.\n\nMake sure to use your [item=craftingguide] Crafting Guide to find out how to make components you don't know.\n\nYou can click them in the guide and the book will automatically navigate to them.
guide28title=Start a settlement
guide28objective=Craft a [item=settlementflag] Settlement Flag and start a new settlement
guide29title=Craft a Carpenter's Bench
guide29objective=Craft a [item=carpentersbench] Carpenter's Bench.
guide30title=Craft a Landscaping Station
guide30objective=Craft a [item=landscapingstation] Landscaping Station.
guide31title=Craft a Job Request Bulletin
guide31objective=Craft a [item=jobrequestbulletin] Job Request Bulletin.
guide32title=Help Wanted
guide32objective=Some specialist workers will only visit your settlement if you procure their assistance.\n\nPlace a [item=jobrequestbulletin] Job Request Bulletin on any wall in your settlement and interact with it to advertise new job positions.\n\nWhen you do, they will sporadically start visiting your settlement.\n\nYou can stop advertising job positions by interacting with or removing the bulletin.\n\nPlace your bulletin to complete this objective.
guide33title=Create a Crafting Task Board
guide33objective=Automated settler crafting was reworked in Dynamic Settlements.\n\nAny settler can craft on workstations, but Carpenter's Benches require Carpenters, Anvils require Blacksmiths, and Alchemy Stations require Alchemists.\n\nCreate a [item=craftingtaskboard] Crafting Task Board to learn more.
guide34title=Seeing danger
guide34objective=Craft the [item=malignancegoggles] Malignance Goggles.

[journalguide]
categorytitle=Dynamic Settlements
earlyhealthtitle=Growing Stronger
earlyhealthbody=You don't feel healthy. Complete the goals below to grow stronger.
earlyhealthkills=Kill hostile mobs
earlyhealthore=Mine copper, iron, or gold ore rocks
earlyhealthfoods=Eat different foods (no raw meat)
earlyhealthcave=Spent time in caves without dying
journalguide22=Let's cook some clay
journalguide22title=Let's cook some clay
journalguide22body=In Dynamic Settlements you can make Charcoal using a Charcoal Pit, and cook Bricks and Molds using a Clay Firing Pit. They work similarly, by digging holes and filling them with the items you want to process.\n\nDig three [item=shallowholetile] holes. In the first hole, place [input=mouse2] a stack of 32 <logicon> logs. Afterwards, cover the hole with a [item=dirtpile] pile of dirt. Use a [item=firestarter] firestarter on the covered hole to set it on fire. This will produce charcoal.\n\nFor the second and third holes, fill them up with [item=unfiredbrick] bricks and <moldicon> molds by placing them [input=mouse2] one by one (Maximum 8 per hole). Afterwards, add 16 <logicon> logs on top, and set them on fire as previously.\n\nThe cooking process will take an amount of time equivalent to a day and night cycle, starting from when they were ignited.\n\nUse that time to collect as much [item=ironore] iron ore as you can, and gather some food if you need.\n\nComplete this objective by retrieving the items below.
journalguide25=Let's cook together
journalguide25title=Let's cook together
journalguide25body=A core feature of Dynamic Settlements is Workstation Linking. Lets start easy, with an example scenario.\n\nTo craft a metal plate in an anvil, it is required to heat up the metal, and shape it while softer. We call this a Forge Requiring Recipe, or FRR for short.\n\nTo prepare for crafting a FRR, all you need to do is open the anvil UI and link the anvil to a nearby forge. This allows you to craft FRRs.\n\nTo proceed, craft a [item=metalworkhammer] Metalworking hammer.
completedbutton=Completed
continuebutton=Continue
requireditems=Required items:
lockedcontainerstitle=Locked Containers
lockedcontainersbody=It seems most, if not all, containers found while exploring are locked and firmly secured to the ground.\n\nYou could remove one with a strong enough tool, but opening it requires a proper Key. Different containers may require different Keys.\n\nWith the right tools and materials you can make Keys yourself, or have a skilled Blacksmith make them for you.\n\nSome containers are tied shut rather than locked. Those can be cut open with a strong enough tool.
lockedcontainersfindkey=Obtain any key
primitivecraftingtitle=Working by Hand
primitivecraftingbody=Crafting in Dynamic Settlements has changed a bit. Many items now require extra components to be crafted.\n\n[item=oaklog] Logs can be placed on the ground and cut into Planks with an axe, and Planks can be cut further into [item=woodenshaft] Wooden Shafts. As you progress you will find many more materials that need to be shaped, cast, fired, or assembled before they are ready to use.\n\nCrafting also takes time.
hidingholestitle=Hiding Underground
hidingholesbody=A [item=shallowholetile] Shallow Hole covered by a [item=trapdoor] Trapdoor can be used as a hiding place. Climb inside and close the Trapdoor above you, and most enemies will lose sight of you.\n\nThis can save your life early on, but remember that a covered hole cannot be used for other purposes until the Trapdoor has been removed.
moldsanddurabilitytitle=Nothing Lasts Forever
moldsanddurabilitybody=Molds can be used up to 10 times until they break.\n\nOther items in Dynamic Settlements may also use durability in different ways. For example, you can see how much time you have left on Crude Torches before they run out.\n\nIf you find a mage try asking them to enchant one of your molds.
moldenchantingtitle=Unbreakable
moldenchantingbody=Wizards can enchant Molds with Unbreaking. An Unbreaking Mold will no longer lose durability when used in recipes.
componentcraftingtitle=Building Better Equipment
componentcraftingbody=You now know the basics of Dynamic Settlements crafting, but more advanced equipment will require more advanced parts.\n\nWeapons, tools and armor may need things such as Blades, Tool Heads, Plates, Panels and Shafts, made from the appropriate materials, before the finished item can be assembled.\n\nYou don't need to memorize all of them. Use the crafting guide whenever you are unsure what parts or stations a recipe requires, or what can be crafted from a particular component.
foundstationstitle=Learn from experience
foundstationsbody=Finding an advanced crafting station does not mean you know how to use it yet.\n\nBefore naturally generated workstations of a new tier become useful to you, you must first learn how they work by crafting one yourself.\n\nOnce you have reached the required tech level and have crafted that station, you will be able to normally use equivalent stations found while exploring.
treasureshoveltitle=Digging Deeper
treasureshovelbody=Your early Shovels were good enough to dig holes on the surface, but cave floors are much harder to break through.\n\nThe [item=treasureshovel] Treasure Shovel is strong enough to dig new holes underground. I wonder how far you can dig...
farmchangestitle=Life on the Farm
farmchangesbody=Farming has a few extra considerations in Dynamic Settlements.\n\nHarvesting mature crops can provide useful [item=grass] Grass and [item=dirtpile] Dirt Piles in addition to the crop itself.\n\nBe careful what you allow to walk through your fields. Hostile creatures, raiders and most larger animals can trample farmland and destroy growing crops. Chickens and similarly small animals are light enough not to cause trouble.\n\nAdult Chickens can provide [item=feather] Feathers, and Apiaries can produce [item=honeycombfragment] Honeycomb Fragments that are useful for further crafting.

woodashfarmlandtitle=Wood Ash and Farmland
woodashfarmlandbody=Wood Ash [item=woodash] is a byproduct of charcoal and firing pits.\nIt can be combined with dirt and fertilizer to craft farmland soil.
settlementintrotitle=Settlement Dynamics
settlementintrobody=Settlements can do much more in Dynamic Settlements than they normally can.\n\nAs your settlement grows you can recruit specialist workers like Builders and Carpenters, manage stocked supplies, automate crafting and production, construct entire buildings from Blueprints, organize proper defences, and expand your settlement underground.\n\nYou don't need to learn everything at once. New Journal entries will appear as you encounter these systems.
happinessworktitle=Happy Workers Work Better
happinessworkbody=Happiness affects how efficiently your workers perform. Happy settlers can carry more items while working and move faster while hauling them. Happy Builders will perform construction and repairs faster. Unhappy settlers will still do their jobs, but large amounts of material will take much longer to move around.\n\nKeeping your workers comfortable can make a very noticeable difference in a busy settlement.
builderstitle=Builders
buildersbody=Builders specialize in construction and maintenance.\n\nThey can construct designs saved in Blueprints, collecting the necessary materials from settlement storage and clearing ordinary obstacles along the way.\n\nBuilders will also repair damaged structures around your settlement. Keep their Construction work priority enabled if you want them to take care of these jobs.\n\nRain and snow can damage your settlement's exposed constructions over time. You will need Builders to repair them before they break apart completely.\n\nDifferent materials have different weather damage resistance. You can inspect their resistance by wearing the [item=inspectionglass] Inspection Glass.
blueprintcreationtitle=Capturing a Build
blueprintcreationbody=A Blueprint records an area of the world so your Builders can recreate it later.\n\nSelect the area containing the structure you want to save. Before finishing, you can exclude tile or object types that you do not want copied.\n\nBlueprints can remember complex builds, including wiring and logic components and layered objects like torches over tables.\n\nThe Blueprint stores the design only. The materials still need to be supplied when it is built.
blueprintconstructiontitle=Building from Blueprints
blueprintconstructionbody=Once a Blueprint project is placed, your Builders will begin turning the preview into the real structure.\n\nThey will collect the required materials from settlement storage and remove ordinary objects that are in the way. Several Builders can work on the same project together.\n\nIf construction is blocked for any reason, an error message will explain what the problem is. It may be missing materials, something that cannot be removed, an object without proper support, or another condition preventing construction.\n\nThe Blueprint itself is not consumed. You can reuse the same design as many times as you want.
blueprinttoolstitle=Managing Your Plans
blueprinttoolsbody=The Blueprint Workstation gives you more control over designs you have already captured.\n\nYou can rename a Blueprint, remove unwanted tile or object types from it, and copy or paste its Blueprint data to share designs with other players.
projectmanagementtitle=Canceling a Construction Project
projectmanagementbody=Not every project needs to be finished.\n\nA [item=projecteraser] Project Eraser can cancel an active Blueprint project. Anything your Builders have already constructed will remain in the world; only the unfinished project is removed.
inspectionglasstitle=Inspecting Objects
inspectionglassbody=The [item=inspectionglass] Inspection Glass can be used to inspect objects placed in the world, revealing their current health/damage and their weather resistance tier.\n\nThe pips on the left side show the resistance tier from 0 to 4, and the bar on the right shows the current object's health value.\n\nEach resistance tier increases the amount of time objects take to be damaged by weather, and tier 4 is completely immune to weather damage.\n\nThe Inspection Glass can also be used to show or hide in-progress Blueprint project previews when they get in the way.\n\nHint: Ask a Builder to join you in an Adventure Party to get some more information on weather damage.
travellingbuildertitle=A Builder on the Road
travellingbuilderbody=Constructions built in the wilderness, outside settlements, will also take weather damage. You can build temporary shelters that will eventually crumble.\n\nIf you want your wilderness buildings to be permanent, you must bring Builders with you.\n\nTalk to them and ask them to make improvements on the road. They will improve blocks you place so they become permanent. You can use the [item=inspectionglass] Inspection Glass to check whether a block has already been improved.
stockmanagementtitle=Keep This Chest Stocked
stockmanagementbody=Sometimes a storage container should always keep certain supplies close at hand.\n\nIn a settlement chest configuration screen, click "Advanced" to show Stock settings. These settings allow you to choose an item and tell your settlers how many should be kept in that container. If the amount falls below the "Restock" value, settlers will bring more from your other settlement storage until the target is restored.\n\nItems being kept in stock and below the stock limit are protected from ordinary hauling so settlers cannot remove those items from that chest.\n\nPlayers can still manually remove stocked items. Chests linked to crafting stations as Input Storage also allow automatic crafting to use stocked items.\n\nStock settings overrule chest priority settings: a chest that needs stock will create a stock-up job from any settlement chest of any priority.
carpenterstitle=Carpenters
carpentersbody=Carpenters are specialist crafting workers.\n\nThey are the only settlers capable of using the Carpenter's Bench for automated crafting.\n\nTo make full use of them, you will want to learn how Workstation Storage Links and Crafting Task Boards work.
workstationlinkstitle=Feeding a Workshop
workstationlinksbody=Automated crafting stations don't use items from settlement chests directly for crafting. They instead take their ingredients from Input Storage and place products in Output Storage.\n\nUse the station interface to link input and output containers if you want to use settlers for crafting automation.\n\nInput chests don't need to be configured as settlement chests, but doing so is useful because Stock settings can keep frequently used ingredients supplied.
craftingtaskstitle=Crafting Tasks
craftingtasksbody=[item=craftingtaskboard] Crafting Task Boards allow you to tell your settlers what a workstation should produce.\n\nYou can change the order of tasks, pause them, resume them, or remove them whenever your production needs change.\n\nIf a task cannot continue, the Board will tell you what is stopping it, such as missing ingredients or nowhere to put the result.\n\nUnfinished tasks run one at a time until the last one, then loop back to the first. Priority tasks are executed repeatedly until finished before regular tasks continue.
craftingautostocktitle=Let Them Fetch It
craftingautostockbody=You do not need to keep every workshop supplied by hand.\n\nWhen Auto Stocking is enabled, a crafting station can request missing ingredients from the rest of your settlement storage. Settlers will bring those ingredients to linked Input Storage when a task needs them.\n\nThis is a temporary solution because it can cause many hauling trips. Ideally, frequently used ingredients should be supplied through more efficient means.
automatedmetalworkingtitle=An Automated Smithy
automatedmetalworkingbody=Metalworking can be automated just like other settlement crafting.\n\nBlacksmiths can operate Anvils, process materials in Forges, and perform Forge Requiring Recipes through linked workstations.\n\nThe same rules still apply: a Forge Requiring Recipe needs an appropriate linked Forge, and that Forge needs enough Charcoal to provide the heat.
charcoalautomationtitle=Charcoal Production
charcoalautomationbody=The Charcoal Pit process you learned earlier can eventually be handed over to your settlers.\n\nCreate a Charcoal Production Zone and workers can prepare pits inside it, bring Logs, cover them with Dirt, ignite them, and collect the finished Charcoal when it is ready.\n\nYou can tell the zone to maintain a desired amount of Charcoal, or let it keep producing continuously.
clayautomationtitle=Clay Firing
clayautomationbody=Settlers can automate Clay Firing Pits.\n\nCreate a Clay Firing Zone and your workers can bring the unfired items and Logs, load the pits, ignite them, and retrieve the finished products once firing is complete.\n\nEach type of fired item can have its own desired stock amount, or it can be set to continue producing without a limit.
fishingareastitle=Where to Cast a Line
fishingareasbody=You can let your Anglers fish wherever they want, but you can also create Fishing Areas to control exactly where they are allowed to work.\n\nThis allows settlements that span multiple biomes to assign Anglers to particular biomes and optimize which fish are caught.\n\nYou can even create Fishing Areas inside caves to catch cave-exclusive fish.
settlementdefencetitle=Walls Are Not Invincible
settlementdefencebody=A fence is useful, but it is not an invisible wall.\n\nZombies can attack doors, fences and other barriers when those structures stand between them and something they want to reach. Some enemies may even attack your animals.\n\nIf you want a settlement to survive serious attacks, build its defences with the expectation that enemies may eventually try to break through them.\n\nHint: Craft a [item=warningbell] Warning Bell to learn more about defences and complete this Journal entry.
warningbellstitle=Sound the Alarm
warningbellsbody=[item=warningbell] Warning Bells can help Guards respond to attacks before your walls are already gone.\n\nYou can place Bells on fences or in the immediate wall next to a door. If an attack happens on that door, or any fence connected up to 50 fences away from the placed Bell, a priority warning will ring and alert Guards in your settlement. They will abandon other tasks and run to defend the attacked structure. A barrier that has actually been breached is treated as an even more urgent threat.
guarddutytitle=Night gathers, and now my watch begins
guarddutybody=Hostile mobs can attack your settlement during the night. Zombies can, for instance, detect and attack farm animals and breach fences.\n\nGuards can be assigned to Day Duty or Night Duty.\n\nNight Guards patrol while most settlers sleep, and sleep during the day. Day Guards follow the opposite routine.\n\nWhatever schedule you choose, Guards still need time to eat, relax and sleep. A Guard who never gets proper rest will eventually pay for it.
guardfatiguetitle=Let Them Rest
guardfatiguebody=Being repeatedly dragged out of bed to fight is exhausting.\n\nWhen a Guard's scheduled sleep is interrupted by combat, they will build up Fatigue. Tired Guards move more slowly, fight less effectively, and become easier to injure. If things get bad enough they may become unable to keep fighting at all.\n\nEvery day they get proper sleep will reduce their Fatigue level.
malignancetitle=Seeing Danger
malignancebody=Malignance Goggles let you see something that normally remains hidden: places where hostile creatures are able to spawn.\n\nWear them while inspecting your settlement to find unsafe rooms, dark corners, cave sections, or other places where enemies may appear.\n\nIf something keeps spawning where you do not expect it, the Goggles are a good place to start looking for the problem.
sleepingtitle=The Settlement Never Sleeps
sleepingbody=Sleeping does not simply freeze the world until morning in Dynamic Settlements.\n\nYou cannot sleep during the day, and when sleeping at night, the world continues to advance while you rest.\n\nThat means important events can happen while you are asleep. Your settlement may be attacked, your animals slaughtered, and you may find a very different settlement come morning.\n\nCheck your settlement's sleep settings to learn more.
sleepalarmstitle=Wake Me If Something Happens
sleepalarmsbody=Settlement Sleep Settings let you decide what is important enough to wake you up.\n\nYou can ask to be awakened when a Raid begins, when zombies start attacking your settlement barriers, or only when those barriers are actually breached.\n\nWhen possible, your Guards will handle raising the alarm. If you have complete confidence in your Guards, you can ask them to leave you alone and deal with those pesky raiders themselves.
cavesettlementstitle=Expanding Underground
cavesettlementsbody=Your settlement does not have to stop at the surface.\n\nA cave directly beneath your settlement can become another level of that same settlement. To connect the two, assign one or more Cave Ladders as settlement access routes.\n\nSettlers will only use the Cave Ladders you have approved, giving you control over where they travel between levels.\n\nA settlement can be contained entirely on the surface, entirely in a cave, or across both levels.\n\nWork zones can be assigned independently on each level. Settlers can have a bed on either level, and Guards can be assigned to patrol either level.
caveresidentstitle=Living Underground
caveresidentsbody=Settlers can live underground as well as on the surface.\n\nBeds placed in suitable cave rooms can be assigned normally. Settlers can also have preferences about whether they would rather live on the Surface or Underground. Living on a different level is not directly detrimental, but they receive a happiness boost when allowed to live where they prefer.\n\nBe careful about leaving settlers stranded in caves with no food and no access to the surface; they will not last long under those conditions.
caveinfrastructuretitle=One Settlement, Two Levels
caveinfrastructurebody=The surface and cave belong to the same settlement, but many things are configured separately on each level.\n\nStorage, Workstations, work zones, restriction zones and Fishing Areas can all be assigned underground without interfering with their surface versions.\n\nYour settlement still shares its people and overall resources, but each level can have its own layout and purpose.
crosslevellogisticstitle=Up the Ladder, Down the Ladder
crosslevellogisticsbody=Once your levels are connected, settlers can work across both of them.\n\nThey can haul items between Surface and Cave Storage, travel to Workstations on another level, and perform many ordinary production jobs wherever they are needed.\n\nThe route still matters. If you disable or remove the Ladder they need, the worker or resources on the other side may become unreachable.
crosslevelcommandstitle=Orders Across the Settlement
crosslevelcommandsbody=Linked settlement levels also work with direct settler commands.\n\nSettlers can travel between the surface and cave when following you, moving to a Guard position, responding to combat orders, or travelling as part of your Adventure Party.\n\nAs with normal jobs, they still need at least one assigned ladder available between the levels.
caveguardstitle=Guarding Below
caveguardsbody=A Guard does not have to patrol the same level where they happen to sleep.\n\nYou can assign Guards to protect the Surface or Underground portions of your settlement. Their level assignment is separate from whether they work Day Duty or Night Duty.\n\nUse both settings together if you want reliable protection across a large multi-level settlement.
undergroundthreatstitle=It Follows
undergroundthreatsbody=Moving underground does not make your settlement untouchable.\n\nZombies and other suitable ground enemies can detect targets through connected settlement levels and may use your ladders to pursue them in either direction.\n\nBe careful: hostile mobs can use any ladders regardless of whether they are designated as settlement ladders. They don't care about your rules.
multilevelraidstitle=Raids Below Ground
multilevelraidsbody=Raiders see your surface and underground settlement as parts of the same target.\n\nA Raid may threaten both levels and divide its forces according to your wealth distribution.\n\nIf raiders see tempting targets underground they can dig into the cave and attack from below, so make sure every level that matters has proper defences.

starvationtitle=Starving Settlers
starvationbody=Settlers need a steady supply of food. If a settler's Hunger reaches zero, they will begin taking starvation damage and cannot recover health until they eat again. If starvation continues, they will eventually collapse and become downed, requiring revival like any other downed settler.\n\nA settler stranded underground is in even greater danger. If they have no accessible food and no usable settlement route back to the surface, reaching zero Hunger will down them immediately. Keep cave residents supplied, or make sure they have a working way back to the surface.
interfacecontrolstitle=Interface Shortcuts
interfacecontrolsbody=Dynamic Settlements adds a few QoL interface shortcuts.\n\nHold Ctrl and use the mouse wheel to change the screen zoom. Ctrl with Numpad + or Numpad - changes the interface scale.\n\nAnd you can press pause on your keyboard to pause your game in single player.\n\nIf these controls conflict with another mod or your preferred setup, they can be disabled in the Dynamic Settlements settings.

[enchantment]
dsunbreaking=Unbreaking
